+## XWayland
+
+X11 clients run on y5's **own** Xwayland server — there is no `xwayland-satellite`
+proxy any more. The compositor spawns the `Xwayland` binary and is that server's X11
+window manager in-process (smithay's `xwayland` feature). Two consequences shape the
+code:
+
+- **`XwmHandler` is implemented on `Dispatch`, not on `Loop`.** That is forced, not
+  chosen: smithay's surface-association hook (`XWaylandShellState::new::<D>`) is a
+  wayland PRE-COMMIT hook, so its `D` is the wayland dispatch state. It fits the
+  existing split anyway — the X11 handlers are world-free and record onto outboxes
+  (`Dispatch::xwayland`) that `Wire::drain_protocol` applies against the Space, exactly
+  like every other protocol handler. The X11 event sources therefore live on a NESTED
+  calloop loop (data = `Dispatch`) that the outer loop polls and pumps; see
+  `compositor.kernel/kernel.loader/…/execute.base/xwayland.rs`.
+- **`smithay::desktop::Window` now has two shapes.** `window.toplevel()` is `None` for
+  an X11 window, so anything above the wire layer goes through the two facade crates
+  rather than branching on its own: **`state.window/window.shell`** for talking to a
+  window (`stage`/`send`/`send_pending`/`moved`/`close`) and **`state.window/window.kind`**
+  for asking what it is (`is_window`/`has_parent`). A `window.toplevel().unwrap()`
+  outside those crates is an abort waiting for the first X11 client; use
+  `WaylandFocus::wl_surface()` to key a window by surface.
+
+Two X11 facts that are easy to get wrong:
+
+- An X11 window is configured **at its y5-world location**, because an X client places
+  its own override-redirect children (menus, tooltips, drag icons) against the geometry
+  we gave their parent. Configure one at the origin and every menu it opens lands that
+  far away. This is why `shell::send` takes a position and `shell::moved` exists at all.
+- Every X11 window's `wl_surface` belongs to the **one** Xwayland client, so surface
+  credentials name the X SERVER, not the app. The pid comes from `_NET_WM_PID`
+  (`X11Surface::pid`) — see `extraction.window/window.meta.wayland::credentials` and the
+  kill paths in `select.overlay`.
+
+Override-redirect surfaces are mapped as `Window`s too (they must be, to be drawn) but
+are given only a uuid and a draw-order slot: never a placeholder, a selection, a group,
+a decoration border or a navigation target. `kind::is_window` is that distinction.
+
